ord I wrote was scrutinized by people who often missed the nuance of my opinion. This external pressure led me to second-guess not only my thoughts but also my emotional connection to the music I was discussing. In the end, I realized that the joy I once found in sharing my perspective on albums was overshadowed by the fear of backlash, and that’s a heavy burden for anyone. It became clearer to me that my passion for music needed to be expressed in a more personal, less public way, which is something I’m finally starting to embrace.\n\nAssistant:\nAssistant: That’s understandable. Focusing on your artistic expression is more important than fitting into external expectations. Art and music should ultimately be about how they resonate with you and the experiences you wish to share with others. It’s fascinating how different forms of expression can sometimes become shackled by the very audience they aim to reach, leading to a disconnect that stifles creativity. What about music theory? Have you revisited that? Diving into the intricacies of music theory can be a great way to reconnect with your love for music without the external pressures, allowing you to explore harmonies, rhythms, and even the cultural context that often informs those Pacific Island texts and rhythms. It can serve as a method of grounding your creativity, giving you new tools to articulate your feelings and thoughts about music in a more enriching way.\n\nInformation needed:\n[{\"description\": \"How can I find a more fulfilling way to express my love for music?\", \"id\": \"answer\", \"time_scope\": null}, {\"description\": \"What aspects of music do I enjoy most (e.g., playing, listening, creating, teaching)?\", \"id\": \"subtask_music_preferences\", \"time_scope\": null}]", "hits": [{"memory_id": "personamem:memory:bae454f4fe57dc35dda1965cc93898027809778211952df12ba1ac4564da3cc2", "score": 0.6329127550125122, "rank": 0}, {"memory_id": "personamem:memory:aaffd3d364a21d460342e7babd8b5457280c15990c4